Scenario ID: S05
Scenario title: Specification Document Review
Initial workflow description:
An engineer reviews a specification document to check whether new features are reflected correctly and whether there are typos or missing details.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition starts the review process — for example, is it triggered automatically when a new document version is submitted, or does someone manually assign the review task to the engineer?",
      "answer": "The process begins when the person doing the review gathers the specification along with the associated list of feature changes. The trigger is not specified as automatic or manual in the workflow, but the first action is the reviewer actively collecting those materials, implying the reviewer initiates the work rather than waiting for an automated assignment."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "In what system and format is the specification document stored and accessed by the reviewer — for instance, a document management platform, version control repository, or email, and is the document structured or free-form text?",
      "answer": "The specification lives in a dedicated storage location for documents, and the reviewer also consults a structured checklist tool. The exact platform type or file format is not specified, but both a central document store and a commenting tool are used during the review process."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Does the engineer follow a defined checklist or set of criteria when reviewing the document, or is the review based on personal judgment and experience?",
      "answer": "The reviewer follows a predefined checklist rather than relying purely on personal judgment. The checklist guides evaluation of feature coverage, consistent use of terminology, and formatting, making the process structured and repeatable rather than ad hoc."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "Besides the reviewing engineer, are there other roles involved in this workflow — such as a document author who receives feedback, a team lead who gives final approval, or a quality assurance role?",
      "answer": "Yes, three additional roles participate: the person who originally wrote the specification and incorporates feedback, the owner of the features being documented who confirms that revisions are technically correct, and a separate approver who gives the final sign-off on the document."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "What are the possible outcomes at the end of the review, and what action is taken for each outcome — for example, if issues are found, is the document sent back for revision, and if it passes, who is notified and what happens next?",
      "answer": "Two decision points exist. First, if feature coverage is incomplete, the document goes back for updates; if complete, editing checks continue. Second, once revisions are submitted, they are either accepted and sent for final sign-off, or rejected and returned for another round of corrections. Successful completion ends with an approved document."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "How does the engineer record and communicate the issues found during the review — for example, are comments added directly in the document, logged in a separate tracking tool, or sent via email — and how is resolution of those issues confirmed?",
      "answer": "The reviewer logs findings using a dedicated commenting tool and a separate tracker for requested changes. Resolution is confirmed when the feature owner validates that the author's updates are technically accurate, after which the corrected document advances to the approver for final acceptance."
    }
  ]
}
